<BaseLayout title="High-Yield Dividend Stocks: Risk & Suitability" description="Research high-yield dividend names by category — traditional dividend stocks, REITs, BDCs, and covered-call ETFs — with the risk and income context each one needs." canonical="https://www.dividend01.com/topics/high-yield-dividend-stocks/" schemaType="WebPage" pubDate="2026-08-07" modDate="2026-08-27">
  <slot slot="title">High-Yield Dividend Stocks</slot>
  <slot slot="subtitle">Higher income can come with higher risk. Learn how to evaluate sustainability before comparing yields.</slot>

  <p>A 7% yield looks attractive next to a 2% one, but the sources of high yield are very different and the risks are very different. A traditional utility trading at a 6% yield because the market expects rate cuts is not the same investment as a covered-call ETF distributing 12% mostly as return of capital, which is not the same as a BDC paying 9% because of its regulated payout structure, which is not the same as a YieldMax single-stock fund paying 60% because it's selling aggressive weekly options. This hub organizes high-yield names by their underlying mechanism, with the risk context each category needs.</p>

  <h2>Reading path by experience</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Beginner</strong> — read the four categories below, then <a href="/articles/high-dividend-stocks/">High Dividend Stocks</a> for sector examples.</li>
    <li><strong>Retiree / income investor</strong> — read the suitability matrix, then model after-tax income with the <a href="/calculator/">Calculator</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Income trader</strong> — read <a href="/articles/ulty-dividend/">ULTY</a> and <a href="/articles/msty-dividend-income/">MSTY</a> for the option-income mechanics, then <a href="/articles/dividend-calculator-guide/">the Calculator Guide</a> for sizing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sub-topic groups by category</h2>

  <h3>1. Traditional high-yield equities (4–8%)</h3>
  <p>Mature, profitable companies paying above the market-average yield, usually in stable sectors like consumer staples, tobacco, energy midstream, telecommunications, and big banks. The yield is real income from operations, but each sector has its own risks.</p>
  <div class="topic-links">
    <a href="/articles/high-dividend-stocks/"><strong>High Dividend Stocks Guide</strong><span>Sector-by-sector breakdown and the 4–8% yield framework.</span></a>
    <a href="/articles/highest-dividend-stocks/"><strong>Highest Dividend Stocks</strong><span>Extreme yields, return of capital, and loss risks.</span></a>
  </div>

  <h3>2. REITs, MLPs, BDCs (structural high yield)</h3>
  <p>These entities are required by their tax structure to distribute most of their taxable income, so double-digit yields are normal. They carry interest-rate sensitivity, sector concentration (real estate, energy, private credit), and complex tax reporting (K-1s).</p>
  <div class="topic-links">
    <a href="/articles/high-dividend-stocks/"><strong>REIT and BDC sections</strong><span>How to read AFFO payout, FFO coverage, and non-accrual ratios.</span></a>
  </div>

  <h3>3. Covered-call and option-income ETFs (7–14%)</h3>
  <p>ETFs that own equities or an index and sell options on top to generate additional income. Distribution yield is real cash flow, but capped upside, NAV erosion from premiums, and tax complexity (Section 1256) all apply.</p>
  <div class="topic-links">
    <a href="/articles/jepi-dividend/"><strong>JEPI Dividend</strong><span>JPMorgan Equity Premium Income ETF, ~7–8%, monthly, equity-linked notes.</span></a>
    <a href="/articles/jepq-dividend-history/"><strong>JEPQ Dividend History</strong><span>Nasdaq-100 version, ~7–8%, monthly.</span></a>
    <a href="/articles/qqqi-dividend/"><strong>QQQI Dividend</strong><span>NEOS Nasdaq-100 option income, ~14%, monthly.</span></a>
    <a href="/articles/spyi-dividend-history/"><strong>SPYI Dividend History</strong><span>NEOS S&amp;P 500 option income, ~12%, monthly.</span></a>
  </div>

  <h3>4. YieldMax single-stock option-income ETFs (50%–100%+ headline)</h3>
  <p>Weekly covered-call strategies on single volatile names, run by YieldMax. Headline distribution rates are extremely high, but most of the distribution is return of capital; total return since launch is roughly flat, and a single-stock ETF is not a diversified dividend holding.</p>
  <div class="topic-links">
    <a href="/articles/ulty-dividend/"><strong>ULTY Dividend</strong><span>Multi-asset YieldMax option-income, ~60% rate, weekly.</span></a>
    <a href="/articles/msty-dividend-income/"><strong>MSTY Dividend Income</strong><span>MicroStrategy-linked, monthly, heavy ROC.</span></a>
    <a href="/articles/tsly-dividend-history/"><strong>TSLY Dividend History</strong><span>Tesla-linked, weekly.</span></a>
