  <h2>Yield vs. total return across categories</h2>
  <p>Yield alone can be misleading because the four categories above have very different total-return profiles. The same 8% yield means very different things in each.</p>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Typical 1Y yield</th><th>Typical 1Y price change</th><th>Typical 1Y total return</th><th>Income quality</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Traditional dividend stock (e.g., MO, VZ)</td><td>+5–7%</td><td>−5% to +10%</td><td>+0% to +15%</td><td>High (qualified dividends)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>REIT (e.g., O, NNN)</td><td>+5–7%</td><td>−10% to +5%</td><td>−5% to +10%</td><td>Medium (ordinary income, but real cash)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Covered-call ETF (e.g., JEPI, SPYI)</td><td>+8–12%</td><td>Tracks index, capped</td><td>Index return + premium, less upside</td><td>Medium-high (Section 1256 favorable)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>YieldMax single-stock (e.g., ULTY, MSTY)</td><td>+50–100%</td><td>Sharply negative</td><td>Roughly 0% since launch</td><td>Low (mostly ROC, small real income)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Read the table across the row. The 50% yield in the bottom row and the 5% yield in the top row can deliver very similar 1-year total returns — and the bottom row carries concentrated single-stock risk on top.</p>

  <h2>Risk points specific to high-yield categories</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Yield trap risk (traditional equities).</strong> A 9% yield on a cyclical company often precedes a dividend cut when the cycle turns. Use <a href="/articles/dividend-dividend-stocks/">the screening guide</a> to verify payout ratio and FCF coverage before assuming the high yield is durable.</li>
    <li><strong>Interest-rate sensitivity (REITs, BDCs, utilities).</strong> When bond yields rise, the dividend yield on these names has to compete with risk-free alternatives. A REIT yielding 5% in a 5% rate environment looks very different from the same REIT yielding 5% when bonds pay 2%.</li>
    <li><strong>NAV erosion (option-income ETFs).</strong> The premium the fund collects from selling options is real cash, but it has to come from somewhere. In a flat or down market the fund gives up its dividend income to keep paying the option premium, which over time erodes the NAV per share. The fund can still deliver a positive total return if the underlying index appreciates, but the high distribution is partly a transfer of future returns into current income.</li>
    <li><strong>Capped upside in strong bull markets (covered-call funds).</strong> A covered-call fund gives up part of every strong rally. JEPI and SPYI have outperformed in flat or modestly up markets but trail in strong up markets; over 10+ years the gap can be substantial.</li>
    <li><strong>Single-stock concentration (YieldMax funds).</strong> ULTY is "multi-asset" but still concentrated in high-volatility tech and growth names. MSTY, TSLY, CONY, NVDY are each single-stock funds; a 50% drawdown in the underlying is a 50% drawdown in your holding before any premium collected. Treat as a satellite, not a core.</li>
    <li><strong>Tax complexity.</strong> REITs distribute ordinary income even in retirement accounts (which is fine), but K-1 forms are delayed and may not be supported by every brokerage. Section 1256 funds (SPYI, QQQI) report on Form 1099-B, not 1099-DIV, which most tax software handles correctly. ROC distributions reduce cost basis; if you don't track it, you overpay tax on the eventual sale.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Methodology and data sources</h2>
  <p>Yield numbers in this hub come from each fund's official fund page (YieldMax, NEOS, JPMorgan, ProShares) as of the dates shown in each child's footer. Where official and independent sources differ (e.g., YieldMax's 60.33% distribution rate vs. DividendVision's 62%), we use the official figure and note the independent estimate. ROC percentages come from issuer Section 19(a) notices, which are filed within 60 days of each distribution. Total return figures use split-adjusted series from Robinhood, StockAnalysis, and Tencent's data service, cross-checked against the issuer's monthly fact sheet where available.</p>
  <p>Our <a href="/articles/dividend-calculator-guide/">Dividend Calculator Guide</a> and the <a href="/calculator/">online Calculator</a> let you model after-tax income from each category using your own tax bracket and contribution assumptions.</p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <details class="faq-item" open><summary>What yield is too high?</summary><div class="faq-answer">For diversified operating companies (consumer, industrial, tech), anything above 8% should make you stop and verify with payout ratio, free cash flow coverage, and dividend history. For REITs, MLPs, and BDCs, higher yields are structural and need different filters. For option-income ETFs, headline yield is largely distribution mechanics, not real income.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>Are high-yield ETFs safe to hold long term?</summary><div class="faq-answer">Depends on the category. A traditional REIT or MLP with conservative leverage and a strong sponsor can be a 10+ year holding. A YieldMax single-stock option-income ETF is closer to a trading instrument with a high distribution; total return since launch for most is roughly flat, so the high yield is mostly return of capital, and the underlying concentration means a single bad quarter can wipe out years of distributions.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>Should I hold high-yield names in a taxable or tax-advantaged account?</summary><div class="faq-answer">Tax-advantaged (IRA, 401k, Roth) is simpler. In a taxable account, the tax treatment matters: qualified dividends are taxed low, ordinary (REIT/BDC) at marginal rates, ROC not taxed now but reduces basis, and Section 1256 contracts at 60/40. Use the <a href="/articles/dividend-calculator-guide/">Calculator Guide</a> to compare after-tax income from each structure in your actual bracket.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>Is a 12% yield from SPYI really 12% income?</summary><div class="faq-answer">No. SPYI's 12% distribution rate comes mostly from selling SPX index options. The SEC 30-day yield (which excludes option premiums and only counts actual investment income) is around 0.5%. So roughly 11.5 percentage points of the 12% is option premium and ROC. In a taxable account it gets Section 1256 treatment (60% long-term, 40% short-term); in a tax-advantaged account it's all ordinary. Either way, the cash is real but it's not "yield" in the same sense as a SCHD dividend.</div></details>

